Prove that the Hankel matrix $J_n$ is positive definite for all $n.$

We know that the eigenvalues of $J$ are positive real numbers, with the largest eigenvalue Tending to $\infty$. Then $J$ must have a positive eigenvalue. Is positive definite guaranteed in this case?
We can construct the companion matrix as
$$C=\begin{pmatrix}a_0 & a_1 &  \cdots  &  a_{n-1} \\{1} & 0 &  &  \\ & \ddots&  &\\  &  &  0 &  &{1}\\ &  &  &  a_1 & a_0 \end{pmatrix}.$$
Then, $C^TJ+kI=C^TJ^T+kI=CJ^T+kI=C(J^T+kI),$ so our matrix is positive definite if and only if $k=-a_{n-1}.$  Thus, the matrix is positive definite for all $k\ne -a_{n-1}$ and negative-definite for $k=-a_{n-1}$.

